# lit — a web-first literate programming system

This document is lit's own specification, told as a literate program. lit is a
literate programming system: an author writes a human-readable document first,
then shows how each part of the code comes together. This document holds itself to
that standard.

The specification does not live in one file any more. Each rule lives in the
module that implements it. A module opens with the prose that specifies its job,
then shows the code that meets it. This top-level document holds the parts that
cross every module — the goals, the vocabulary, the architecture — and points to
the module that owns each detail.

## Goals

lit has six goals.

1. Web-first. The primary woven artifact is a multi-page website, not a PDF. Print
   is not a v1 concern.
2. noweb-compatible. Source files are genuine `.nw` files. `notangle` consumes
   them unchanged. lit is language-agnostic because noweb is.
3. Static generator only. lit is the weaver and the tangler. It ships no server.
   Margin commenting is a separate tool with its own spec, `ANNOTATIONS.md`; lit's
   sole obligation to it is annotation-ready HTML (`rewrite.rs`).
4. JSON is the contract. Every HTML page is a pure function of a JSON document. No
   exceptions (`site.rs`).
5. Review loop. An AI agent reads annotations and proposes revisions to the `.nw`
   source (see "The review loop" below).
6. Self-hosting. lit is written in itself: each `.nw` file tangles to its part of
   the Rust crate (see "Self-hosting" below).

## Non-goals

- PDF or print output. A `--tex` backend may come later.
- Pretty-printing or code reformatting. lit never reflows, re-indents, or rewrites
  the bytes a reader sees. Code chunks render exactly as written — noweb's
  position, and lit keeps it. Syntax highlighting only colors those unchanged
  bytes (`highlight.rs`), so this line still stands.
- cweb's `@d` macros. noweb has no equivalent, because `#define` is C-specific.
  Named chunks cover the need.
- A WYSIWYG authoring UI.
- A comment or annotation server. That is a separate tool (`ANNOTATIONS.md`).
- Multi-user real-time collaboration. Comments are asynchronous.

## Vocabulary

| Term | Meaning |
|---|---|
| project | A directory with a `lit.toml`. It holds one or more documents. |
| document | An ordered list of `.nw` files woven into one site with unified numbering. It has an id, used in URLs. |
| chunk | noweb's unit: a docs chunk (prose) or a code chunk (named, tangleable). |
| section | lit's unit of anchoring and rendering. One chunk makes one section. |
| page | An output HTML file. It holds a contiguous run of sections. |
| root | A code chunk that tangles to a file on disk. |

A document may span several `.nw` files, because `markup` accepts several and
emits a file marker between them. The numbering, the labels, and the chunk
resolution are all document-wide.

## Architecture

lit shells out to noweb's front end, not its back end. `markup` converts a `.nw`
file into noweb's line-oriented pipeline format. lit consumes that as its IR and
owns everything downstream. lit never uses `noweave`: it owns prose-to-HTML
regardless, and the pipeline format hands it the chunk structure as data rather
than as generated markup it would have to scrape back.

```
  PHASE 1 — static generator (no Python, no server)

  *.nw ──> markup ──> pipeline IR ──┐
                                    │
   ┌────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────┐
   │ Rust                                                        │
   │  1. parse IR       → chunk list                             │
   │  2. resolve        → chunk graph, defs/uses, continuations  │
   │  3. prose pipeline → math out, HTML parse, refs, ids        │
   │  4. number         → figures, tables, equations, chunks     │
   │  5. split          → pages, nav tree, prev/next             │
   │  6. serialize      → site.json                              │
   │  7. render         → minijinja + templates/                 │
   └────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────┘
                                    │
                              _site/{*.html,*.json,static/}

  *.nw ──> notangle -R'src/main.rs' ──> src/main.rs


  ANNOTATION LAYER — separate tool, separate spec (ANNOTATIONS.md)

  _site/*.html ──(overlay / embed)──> margin comments on woven pages
        │  HTML carries provenance attributes (rewrite.rs)
        ▼
  annotations (W3C JSON) ──> lit review ──> unified diff against *.nw
```

