## The review loop

`lit review` closes the loop from a reader's margin comment back to the source. It
runs in six steps.

1. It fetches the open annotations as W3C Web Annotation JSON from the configured
   backend. A backend is an adapter; the agent codes against the W3C shape only.
2. It maps each annotation to source. The annotation's exact-quote selector locates
   the text on the woven page. The enclosing section's provenance attributes locate
   the `.nw` region.
3. It asks for a unified diff against the `.nw` source — never against the woven
   HTML.
4. It validates the patch: the patch applies cleanly, `lit weave` still succeeds,
   and `lit tangle` output is unchanged unless a thread explicitly asked for a code
   change.
5. It presents a reviewable diff. Nothing auto-commits.
6. On acceptance, the addressed threads resolve with a pointer to the resulting
   revision.

Step 4 holds the important safety property: prose feedback must not silently alter
the program.

## Self-hosting

lit is written in itself. The path had three stages. First, lit was written
normally in Rust. Second, once weave and tangle were stable, each module was ported
into a `.nw` file that tangles into the existing tree; CI runs `lit tangle --check`
to assert the committed source matches what the literate source makes. Third, the
flip: the `.nw` files became the source of truth.

The flip is done. The whole Rust crate and the stylesheet are literate: each
`src/*.rs.nw` and `templates/lit.css.nw` tangles byte-for-byte into its committed
artifact, guarded by `checks.selfhost`. The flake build is two stages. Stage 0
compiles `lit` from the committed seed — the permanent bootstrap, because a tangler
must exist before anything can be tangled. Stage 0 then tangles every `.nw` over the
seed, and stage 1 compiles the shipped binary from that tangled tree. So the shipped
`lit` is built from the `.nw`, not from the hand-checked-in `.rs`. The committed
files are the bootstrap seed, and `checks.selfhost` proves the seed and the tangle
are byte-identical.

The bootstrap must always be buildable without a working `lit`, so the tangled
output stays committed permanently. This is the mitigation for the one risk
self-hosting adds: a weaver bug becomes a bootstrap risk.

The first dogfood is the flake itself. `flake.nix.nw` is lit's own build file told
as a literate program. It tangles byte-for-byte into the committed `flake.nix`,
which is the permanent bootstrap. `checks.dogfood` runs `lit tangle --check` so the
literate source and the bootstrap never drift, and it weaves the page to prove the
Nix grammar colored it — self-hosting the highlighter, which is why Nix is the
second grammar.

## Nix

The build is flake-based. `pkgs.noweb` is a genuine runtime dependency in phase 1,
not merely a test oracle, so the CLI is wrapped to put both `\${noweb}/bin` and
`\${noweb}/lib/noweb` on PATH — the pipeline stages are not on `bin/`. The shape is
deliberately minimal: a plain `rustPlatform.buildRustPackage`, no extra flake
inputs, with crane or a rust overlay held back until a concrete need appears.

The flake carries the checks that guard lit's invariants: a build with the hermetic
cargo tests, a noweb-drift check that diffs `markup` output against the pinned
fixtures, a determinism check that weaves twice and byte-diffs, a tangle
differential against `notangle`, and the self-host and dogfood checks above. Because
a flake sees only git-tracked files, a new file must be added to git before any
`nix` command will see it. The full build lives in `flake.nix.nw`.

## Testing

Each layer has its own strategy.

| Layer | Strategy |
|---|---|
| IR parser | Golden tests over pinned `markup` output; guards against noweb version drift. |
| Prose pipeline | Snapshot tests per stage, especially math-extraction edge cases. |
| Numbering and refs | Property test: every reference resolves; numbers are dense and monotonic. |
| Split | Property test: concatenating all pages' sections reproduces the section list. |
| Tangle | Differential against `notangle` on a fixture corpus. |
| Determinism | Weave twice, diff. |
| Annotation contract | Assert every section carries its id and provenance and a margin slot. |

## Milestones

Phase 1, the static generator, is complete: the flake and IR parser (M0), the prose
pipeline and math (M1), numbering and page splitting and templates (M2), tangle via
notangle (M3), and `site.json` with the manifest and diagnostics (M4).

Phase 2 is annotation integration, where the annotation layer itself is the
separate tool of `ANNOTATIONS.md`: the annotation-ready HTML contract (M5), `lit
review` (M6), and a reserved slot for the annotation-layer fallback (M7).

Phase 3 is done in its shipped slices: the Markdown dialect (M8), stable ids and
the lock file (M11), self-hosting and the flip (M13), and syntax highlighting
(M14). Math macros (M9), citations (M10), and import versus transclude (M12) are the
remaining phase-3 work.

Replacing noweb with a Rust `markup` and `notangle` is not a scheduled milestone. It
is a contingency with explicit triggers, recorded in `tangle.rs.nw`.

## Open questions

- One review patch per run, or per thread? Per-thread accepts granularly but
  conflicts when two threads touch the same prose. The lean is one patch per run
  with per-thread attribution in the diff.
- Should lit watch and rebuild for a local authoring loop? The feedback loop is poor
  without it.

Resolved questions are recorded where they were settled: the two prose dialects live
in `config.rs.nw`, the two-pass rewriter in `rewrite.rs.nw`, and the choice to skip
`noweave` in this document's architecture section.

## Risks

- KaTeX via a JS engine adds build weight and weave latency. The cache mitigates it.
- Self-hosting makes every weaver bug a bootstrap risk. The permanently-committed
  tangle output is the mitigation.
- Re-anchoring quality is the annotation layer's concern, tracked in
  `ANNOTATIONS.md`, because the anchoring is done by its selector machinery rather
  than by heuristics lit invents.

## Error codes

lit reports one diagnostic format from every stage (`diag.rs.nw`). Each code is
stable, so a test or a suppression can name one error and no other. When a module
introduces a code, it claims the next free number in this registry.

| Code | Meaning | Module |
|---|---|---|
| E001 | malformed IR line from `markup` | `noweb.rs` |
| E002 | `markup` failed, `@fatal`, or not found | `noweb.rs` |
| E010 | use of an undefined chunk | `model.rs` |
| E014 | duplicate label | `resolve.rs` |
| E015 | unresolved reference | `resolve.rs` |
| E020 | math render failure | `math.rs` |
| E022 | unterminated math delimiter | `prose.rs` |
| E023 | KaTeX assets missing | `weave.rs` |
| E024 | writing the site failed | `weave.rs` |
| E025 | template render failure | `weave.rs` |
| E026 | rewriting prose HTML failed | `rewrite.rs` |
| E027 | `notangle` failed or not found | `tangle.rs` |
| E028 | writing tangled output failed | `tangle.rs` |
| E029 | `lit.toml` unreadable or malformed | `config.rs` |
| E030 | malformed fenced-float directive | `markdown.rs` |
| E031 | label absent from `lit.lock` under `--locked` | `lock.rs`, `weave.rs` |
| E032 | `lit.lock` unreadable, malformed, or unwritable | `lock.rs` |
| E033 | a highlight key names an unknown language | `main.rs` |
| W102 | chunk defined but never used | `model.rs` |
| W103 | figure or table without a caption | `resolve.rs` |
| W104 | code chunk resolved to no known language | `weave.rs` |

## Conventions

- Dependencies are added reluctantly, one at a time, only when a task needs them.
  Never add one speculatively.
- No panics outside tests. Everything user-facing goes through the diagnostic format
  with a code.
- Every noweb-boundary discovery gets a fixture. A minimal `.nw` plus regenerated
  `.markup` pair and a test is how the verified-noweb prose stays true.
- noweb stays. Do not reimplement `markup` or `notangle`; the removal triggers are
  in `tangle.rs.nw` and none have fired.
- Determinism is a feature. No timestamps, and no HashMap iteration order in output —
  a `BTreeMap` or an explicit sort.
- When a decision conflicts with observed reality, verification wins: probe the real
  binary, record the finding, update the prose.

All `.nw` prose follows ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English: short active
present-tense sentences, one idea each, approved plain verbs, and the reason before
the mechanism.
